# Task 4: Test bench

## Goal
Measure how reliably my alphabet is recognised and show exactly which
glyphs get mistaken for which, so I know what to redesign.

## Mode 1: Drill
- I choose how many repetitions per glyph (default 10).
- The app shows the NAME of a target glyph (not a picture, so I draw
  from memory) in random order across all templates.
- I draw it; the app records { target, predicted, score, timestamp }
  and moves on without telling me if it was right (so I don't adjust
  my drawing to please it).
- At the end, show the results.

## Mode 2: Leave-one-out
- Without drawing anything, test every stored sample against all
  templates with that one sample removed.
- Gives an instant accuracy reading from existing data.

## Results screen (shared by both modes)
- Overall accuracy as a percentage.
- A confusion matrix:
  - rows = what I meant to draw
  - columns = what the app read, plus an "unknown" column
  - each cell shows a count and the row percentage
  - diagonal cells tinted green, off-diagonal cells tinted red by
    how large they are
- Below it, a list of the worst 3 confusions in plain English, e.g.
  "'spiral' was read as 'circle' 30% of the time".
- Tapping any cell shows thumbnails of the drawings in that cell.
- Drill results are saved and included in the export.

## Acceptance checks
- With 4 well-separated glyphs, leave-one-out shows mostly diagonal.
- Deliberately teaching two similar glyphs (e.g. circle and oval)
  shows up clearly as a red off-diagonal pair.
- Tapping that red cell shows me the actual confused drawings.
